    "advanced": {
      "title": "Spelling-Change Verbs — -cer, -ger & Other Stem Changes",
      "explanation": "Several classes of verbs have spelling changes in their present-tense conjugations to preserve pronunciation. (1) -cer verbs: add cedilla (ç) before -ons only: commencer → je commence, nous commençons. (2) -ger verbs: add -e before -ons only: manger → je mange, nous mangeons. (3) Verbs with accented e (e.g., préférer, espérer): in some forms, the e becomes è: je préfère, tu préfères, il préfère, ils préfèrent (but nous préférons, vous préférez). (4) Verbs ending in -yer (payer, employer): some change y to i in forms without a pronounced ending. The most important rule: spelling changes occur only where pronunciation requires them.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "french": "Nous commençons à voyager.",
          "english": "We begin to travel."
        },
        {
          "french": "Vous mangez et vous voyagez.",
          "english": "You eat and you travel."
        },
        {
          "french": "Je préfère atterrir tôt.",
          "english": "I prefer to land early."
        },
        {
          "french": "Nous préférons décoller à midi.",
          "english": "We prefer to take off at noon."
        },
